<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/intermediate/art-beyond-the-west">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Art beyond the West is <strong>most of the world's art history</strong> — with its own
    canons, theories and masterpieces, and its own relationship to the Western museum that
    collected much of it. The comparative frame (the anthropology lesson's method) replaces
    "influence on Europe" as the organising question: each tradition here answers "what can
    images do, and for whom?" with a full, self-consistent system — and the Western answers
    from the last three lessons turn out to be local dialects of a much larger conversation.
    This page walks the syllabus's regions — West and Central Asia, South, East and
    Southeast Asia, Africa, the colonial Americas — and then confronts the frame problem:
    what the words "art", "museum" and "primitive" did to all of it.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Islamic, East Asian, South/Southeast Asian, Africa–Americas–Oceania" widget="equationStep" config={beyondCfg} />

  <h2>Islamic art: the infinite and the word (7th century onward)</h2>
  <p>
    The tradition's signature comes from a negotiated constraint: hadith traditions
    discourage figural imagery in <em>sacred</em> contexts, so the mosque developed the
    supreme alternatives — <strong>calligraphy</strong> (the Quran itself as image: the
    word of God given monumental form, in scripts from Kufic to thuluth) and
    <strong>geometric interlace</strong> (star-and-polygon tessellations built from
    circle constructions — mathematics as contemplation, the infinite patternable
    because the divine is uncircumscribable) — plus the arabesque's endless vegetal
    scrolling. The Dome of the Rock (Jerusalem, 691–92) announced the vocabulary;
    the Alhambra (Nasrid Granada, 13th–14th c.) perfected it — <strong>muqarnas</strong>
    (honeycomb vaulting: three-dimensional stalactite geometry dissolving the ceiling)
    and tilework calibrated to Andalusian light. And the blanket "no images" claim is
    simply false at court: Persian <strong>miniature painting</strong> (the Shahnameh's
    epic illustrations, Safavid workshops) and later Mughal portraiture are among the
    world's great figurative traditions — Islam negotiated images by context, not by
    ban. Materials and techniques of the trade routes: lusterware ceramics (a silver
    sheen fired in reduction — chemistry as luxury), knotted-pile carpets, papermaking
    transmitted west through Central Asia (paper reached Europe through Islamic Spain —
    an interaction with a book revolution attached).
  </p>

  <h2>South and Southeast Asia: the temple as cosmos</h2>
  <p>
    Hindu and Buddhist art encode cosmology in architecture. <strong>Angkor Wat</strong>
    (Cambodia, c. 1113–50) is Mount Meru, the cosmic mountain, in five towers and a
    moat-ocean — the largest religious building on Earth as a diagram of the universe,
    its bas-reliefs narrating both the Mahabharata and King Suryavarman II's own
    processions (history and scripture sharing a wall). <strong>Borobudur</strong>
    (Java, c. 780–840) is the alternate solution: a stepped mandala of 2,672 relief
    panels — the pilgrim circumambulates upward through the life of the Buddha toward
    the void at the top. <strong>Chola bronzes</strong> (South India, 9th–13th c.)
    cast Shiva Nataraja — lord of the dance, creation and destruction in one ring of
    fire — by lost-wax (cire perdue): each bronze unique because the wax original is
    destroyed in casting, an entire theology of impermanence embedded in the technique.
    <strong>Gandhara</strong> (1st–5th c. ce, today's Pakistan-Afghanistan border)
    proves the interaction lens early: Greco-Buddhist sculpture — the first Buddha
    images, wavy hair and Hellenistic drapery on an Indian doctrine, a Silk Road
    synthesis. <strong>Ajanta</strong>'s rock-cut murals (c. 2nd c. bce–480 ce) painted
    bodhisattvas with a fluid grace Renaissance Europe would not meet for another
    thousand years. And the <strong>Mughal synthesis</strong> (16th–17th c.): Persian
    miniature technique and Islamic geometry fused with Indian colour and Hindu
    subjects under Akbar's multi-faith ateliers — the Taj Mahal (1632–53) as the
    tradition's white-marble summary.
  </p>

  <h2>East Asia: landscape as philosophy, painting as time</h2>
  <p>
    China organised its highest art around <strong>shan shui</strong> —
    mountains-and-water — as cosmology: Song-dynasty monuments (Fan Kuan's
    <em>Travelers Among Mountains and Streams</em>, c. 1000; Guo Xi's
    <em>Early Spring</em>, 1072) scale humanity to a few brushstroke dots within the
    cosmic landscape — the Daoist order painted, humans measured by it. The
    <strong>literati ideal</strong> (wenrenhua) made painting a scholar's
    self-cultivation rather than a profession: amateur status as prestige, the
    "three perfections" (poetry, calligraphy, painting) on one sheet, colophons
    accumulating like a centuries-long seminar. The <strong>handscroll</strong>
    (~30 cm tall, metres long) delivers the tradition's deepest difference from the
    West: it is <em>read</em> in time, unrolled arm-span by arm-span right to left —
    closer to film than to the framed picture, with seals and colophons as
    accumulated commentary. Japan specialised and stylised: Zen ink painting
    (Sesshū's splashed landscapes), the tea aesthetic of <strong>wabi-sabi</strong>
    (beauty in the imperfect, the weathered, the transient), and — after peace and
    merchants arrived — <strong>ukiyo-e</strong>, "pictures of the floating world":
    woodblock prints of actors, courtesans and landscapes, mass-produced design at
    astonishing quality (Hokusai's <em>Great Wave</em>, c. 1831 — composition that
    European art would not catch up to until it imported the prints; the previous
    lesson's Japonisme, seen from the producing end).
  </p>

  <h2>Africa: royal courts, ritual function, and the 1897 problem</h2>
  <p>
    The ethnocentrism this subject must dismantle has a founding exhibit: when Leo
    Frobenius saw the <strong>Ife</strong> heads (zinc-brass naturalistic casts,
    12th–15th c., Yoruba Nigeria) in 1910, he concluded they could not be African —
    Atlantis was invoked — because Renaissance-grade naturalism contradicted the
    period's anthropology. They are African, contemporary with Giotto, and the
    counterpart <strong>Benin bronzes</strong> (brass plaques and heads, 13th–19th c.,
    documenting the Edo court's history) were so technically formidable that the
    story repeats. Their modern fame, though, arrives by catastrophe: Britain's 1897
    punitive expedition sacked Benin City and dispersed thousands of objects into
    Western museums — making the bronzes the central case of the
    <strong>repatriation debate</strong>: Germany transferred ownership of over a
    thousand to Nigeria in 2022; the Smithsonian returned its set; the argument now
    runs case by case through provenance and capacity. Africa's broader record
    includes <strong>Great Zimbabwe</strong>'s dry-stone walls, Djenné's adobe mosque
    (the world's largest mud building, replastered as an annual festival), Kongo
    <strong>nkisi nkondi</strong> power figures (nails and blades driven in to
    activate the spirit — sculpture as contract), and the continent's masking
    traditions — which exposes the frame problem at full force: a mask in the
    British Museum is an <em>object</em>; in its community it is a <em>performer</em>
    that appears with dance, music and costume to do specific ceremonial work.
    The word for that difference is function — the syllabus's purpose-and-audience
    lens, and the reason "art versus artefact" is itself a Western artefact.
  </p>

  <h2>The Americas and Oceania</h2>
  <p>
    The pre-contact Americas built monumental traditions the colonial frame long
    demoted. <strong>Maya</strong>: the Bonampak murals (c. 790 ce) — courtly
    narrative cycles in full colour, a painted archive of ritual and war — plus
    screenfold codices (most burned by missionaries; the survivors are the field's
    crown jewels). <strong>Moche</strong> portrait vessels (Peru, 1st–8th c.) model
    individual faces in clay — true portraiture, a millennium before its European
    counterpart noticed; <strong>Paracas</strong> embroidery (Peru) achieved thread
    counts among the finest textiles ever woven. <strong>Northwest Coast
    form-line</strong> (Haida, Tlingit, Kwakwaka'wakw) is a complete, rigorous 2-D
    design system — the ovoid and U-form splitting and rejoining figures across
    house fronts, boxes and Chilkat robes — revitalised by living master carvers
    after the potlatch bans lifted. <strong>Māori whakairo</strong> carving carries
    genealogy in spiral (koru) form on meeting-house interiors — architecture as
    ancestor. And the newest-old movement: <strong>Papunya Tula</strong> (1971,
    Central Australia) translated Dreaming designs — previously sand and body
    painting — into acrylic on board, launching the contemporary Aboriginal art
    movement that is now Australia's most significant visual export: the decisive
    proof that "non-Western" is a geography, never a tense.
  </p>

  <h2>Reading across traditions: the comparative payoff</h2>
  <p>
    Set the traditions side by side and each answers the image-question differently,
    with its own theory: <strong>China</strong> privileges time and the cultivated
    self (the scroll is an experience; the colophon trail is the criticism);
    <strong>Islam</strong> privileges the infinite and the word (pattern as
    contemplation; calligraphy as the supreme art — a "writing civilisation's"
    counterpart to the icon); <strong>India and Southeast Asia</strong> privilege
    the cosmos made architecture (the temple you walk through is the diagram);
    <strong>Africa and Oceania</strong> privilege function and essence (the mask
    performs; the nkisi acts; conceptual completeness outranks optical likeness —
    note the European sculpture debate that echoed this a half-millennium later).
    None is a lesser version of the Western easel picture; each is a full answer.
    The payoff of the comparative method is not a rank order but a lens kit: having
    read purpose, audience, technique and interpretation across ten traditions, you
    can apply them to any tradition — including the one you grew up inside, which
    is where the method bites hardest.
  </p>

  <p>
    One interaction case study belongs to every trade route it travelled:
    <strong>blue-and-white porcelain</strong>. Chinese kilns fired cobalt decoration
    (the pigment imported from Persia — "Muhammadan blue") for export markets from
    the 14th century; Ottoman Iznik re-interpreted the palette on fritware; Safavid
    Persia copied it; Dutch Delft faience imitated it in the 1600s when VOC cargoes
    made it European domestic fashion; and Japan's Arita kilns, seeded by
    Korean potters, captured the European market when Ming supply collapsed. One
    decorative schema, five civilisations, four centuries — none of it "influence
    on the West" and none of it pure: the motif is a shared economy with local
    accents, the cleanest demonstration in material culture that the global
    history of art is a network, not a set of columns in a survey textbook.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>How the "primitive art" category was built — and unmade.</strong> The
    museum wing is a 20th-century invention: colonial expositions (Paris 1907),
    ethnographic collecting, and modernist enthusiasm (Picasso, Vlaminck, Kirchner
    and the "discovery" of African and Oceanic sculpture) created a category —
    "primitive art" — that simultaneously honoured the objects' formal power and
    denied them history, authorship and theory. The unmaking came slowly: Malraux's
    "imaginary museum" universalised the frame, then postcolonial scholarship
    (and artists from the traditions themselves) reattached context, date, maker
    and name — the shift from "anonymous, timeless, tribal" to "artist, school,
    century" is the historiography lesson operating in real time. The catalogue
    language change ("art of Africa" replacing "primitive art"; makers' names
    researched and restored) is the theory's footprint in the accession record.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The repatriation state of play.</strong> The honest summary: contested,
    uneven, and no longer theoretical. Documented-spoliation cases (Benin 1897;
    the Parthenon marbles, removed 1801–12 under a disputed Ottoman firman)
    proceed on different logic than objects acquired by purchase or gift, and
    museums now navigate between 1970 UNESCO conventions, national laws, and
    community claims. The live questions are hard on both sides: universal-access
    arguments (the encyclopedic museum) against provenance justice; conservation
    capacity and politics at the origin against custody ethics in the West.
    What has changed for good: provenance research is now core museum work,
    ownership transfers have precedent (Benin, 2022), and "return or lend" is a
    public question — the discipline's live reckoning, worth following as it
    happens.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How to write comparative art history without a
    Western yardstick (the current answer — polycentric surveys, regional theories
    on their own terms — is an active construction, not a settled method)? What
    does the digital return of collections (open-access images of every Benin
    plaque) do to ownership arguments? And how will the living traditions —
    calligraphers, Haida carvers, Papunya Tula painters, contemporary African
    and Chinese artists now dominating biennales — rewrite the survey's endings?
    The field's best guess: the next edition of "art beyond the West" will not
    need the title — which is the point of teaching it this way now.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Rawson, The Art of Southeast Asia and non-Western survey literature; Visona et al., A History of Art in Africa; Hickling, Islamic art surveys; Coe, The Maya; British Museum and German Contact Point for Collections from Colonial Contexts documentation on Benin (2022); the anthropology lesson's archaeology and ethics.</p>

